index 30d213a..a81d8df 100644
--- a/AGENTS.md
+++ b/AGENTS.md
@@ -67,6 +67,9 @@ ## Core Runtime Contracts
 - The WiFi control thread admits one client at a time: a `ClientConnect` from a second address
   while a client is connected is refused (logged at most every 5 s per address); the connected
   address may always reconnect. `ClientAdmission.h` holds the pure decision and its tests.
+- The in-process hardware VideoToolbox session runs with low-latency rate control
+  (`encoder_low_latency`, default true): no look-ahead and no periodic IDR, keyframes come from
+  `ForceKeyFrame`. The relaxed retry drops it together with the hardware requirement.
 - Vulkan streaming snapshots released color images with application-dispatched Vulkan functions
   into bounded exportable images. Queue submission is non-blocking; fence waits belong to the
   encoder worker. Missing export support, exhausted slots, or wait failures drop the streaming frame
diff --git a/CHANGES.md b/CHANGES.md
index b77a903..6fc03f0 100644
--- a/CHANGES.md
+++ b/CHANGES.md
@@ -46,6 +46,12 @@ ### Added
 
 ### Changed
 
+- The in-process hardware VideoToolbox session enables low-latency rate control
+  (`kVTVideoEncoderSpecification_EnableLowLatencyRateControl`), new config key
+  `encoder_low_latency` (default true). Measured on an M5 Pro at 2272x1264 H.265 72 Hz: the
+  encode callback fell from 12.6 ms to 8.0 ms, and that 8 ms is flat against bitrate (25/50),
+  resolution scale (0.5/0.75), codec (H.264/H.265), preset and refresh rate (72/90). Low-latency
+  mode has no periodic IDR; keyframes come from client requests via `ForceKeyFrame`.
 - The in-process VideoToolbox session now requires the hardware encoder when VideoToolbox reports
   one for the negotiated codec, retrying without the requirement only if that create fails, and the
   runtime log states whether the session it got is hardware or software.
diff --git a/runtime/oxrsys-runtime.toml b/runtime/oxrsys-runtime.toml
index aa56c62..b9400f5 100644
--- a/runtime/oxrsys-runtime.toml
+++ b/runtime/oxrsys-runtime.toml
@@ -48,6 +48,11 @@ video_codec = "h265"
 # quality  = best visual quality, slightly higher latency
 encoder_preset = "balanced"
 
+# VideoToolbox low-latency rate control for the hardware encoder: no look-ahead and no
+# periodic keyframes (clients request them on loss). Cuts the encode callback from ~12.6 ms
+# to ~8.0 ms at 2272x1264 H.265 (M5 Pro, 2026-10-17). Set false to compare.
+encoder_low_latency = true
+
 # Encode HEVC Main10 for H.265 clients that advertise 10-bit decode support.
 # H.264 and clients without the capability continue to receive 8-bit video.
 encoder_10bit = false
